WEBVTT

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.830
We've added in
functionality so that we can

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:11.020
actually have the player
run into our power-up,

00:00:11.021 --> 00:00:16.880
and then on our player game object, you
can see that has power-up turns to true.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:21.400
Now this power-up will be very
useful only for a specific circumstance.

00:00:21.401 --> 00:00:24.309
When our player runs
into one of our spawned

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:27.640
enemies on our screen,
while we have the power-up.

00:00:27.641 --> 00:00:31.000
Now one thing we want to make sure
we do, just to make our lives a bit easier,

00:00:31.001 --> 00:00:37.680
is if we go into our pre-fab's folder,
if we double-click on our enemy,

00:00:37.681 --> 00:00:44.172
we want to set their tags
to be enemy, so we'll add

00:00:44.173 --> 00:00:50.520
a tag, make sure you add
a custom tag for enemy,

00:00:50.521 --> 00:00:58.000
and then go back to the
enemy under the pre-fab window,

00:00:58.001 --> 00:01:07.200
go to the tag section, add the enemy tag,
and then we can leave our pre-fab window.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.720
So now we have a tag for our enemy.

00:01:09.721 --> 00:01:12.713
Next we're going to go
into our player controller

00:01:12.714 --> 00:01:15.840
script, and in our
player controller script,

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:21.520
we're actually going to create a new
method underneath On Trigger Enter,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:30.640
so we're going to make an
On Collision Enter method.

00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:35.440
Now the reason we actually want to use
On Collision Enter versus On Trigger Enter

00:01:35.589 --> 00:01:39.456
is because On Trigger
Enter is useful when you are

00:01:39.457 --> 00:01:43.440
just trying to understand
triggers between colliders.

00:01:43.441 --> 00:01:49.000
But if you're trying to do something with
physics, you use On Collision Enter instead.

00:01:49.001 --> 00:01:54.520
In our On Collision Enter
method, we're going to check again

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.560
for another condition, so
we need an F statement.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:14.323
So if the object we're colliding
with is an enemy, and if

00:02:14.373 --> 00:02:19.960
our player has the power up,
then we should do something.

00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:24.000
In this case, we're just going to
use a debug.log statement for now,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.520
so that way we know that it worked.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.840
So if we debug.log, so that
way we can see in our console,

00:02:32.841 --> 00:02:40.120
now we can type in a string,
so we'll say collided with.

00:02:40.121 --> 00:02:44.360
And now what we're actually going to
do is something called concatenation.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:50.840
So we can actually add strings together to
be able to create one entire full message.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.800
So we have this first string that we
typed out literally using our quotes.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:59.000
And now we actually want to know
the name of what we've collided with,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:05.840
so we would get the
collision.gameobject.name.

00:03:05.841 --> 00:03:10.080
And you see when we use
.name, we actually get a string back.

00:03:10.081 --> 00:03:12.920
And then we use another plus sign.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:19.080
And then another set of
quotes for a literal string again.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.280
With power up, set two.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:28.080
And then if we do a plus,
and then we use has power up.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.560
So this will tell us, when we've collided
with whatever we collide with an enemy,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.480
it will tell us that our power
up is set to true or false.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.160
In this case, it'll be true because
we've added that in our if statement.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:48.040
So you can actually use variables and
strings in an entire message for a debug.log.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:53.800
So that way we have a string,
and then you add in another string,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.080
and then you add in the
string literal once again.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.360
And then you can even add in different
variables like Booleans or integers or floats.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.560
If you're looking for
some precise information.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:12.040
So now when we save this,
so in Unity, when we press play,

00:04:12.279 --> 00:04:20.279
if we pick up the power up, and then
we hopefully collide with our enemy,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.825
there you go. On the
bottom, you can see collided

00:04:22.837 --> 00:04:25.000
with enemy clone with
power ups at the true.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:29.560
Or if we check our console window,
you can see it up there as well.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:35.160
So now it's your turn, you're
going to go into your enemy prefab.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.240
Make sure you apply the enemy tag.

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:43.221
In our player controller script,
make sure that you add an

00:04:43.246 --> 00:04:47.480
on collision enter method
into your player controller script.

00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:53.400
In that on collision enter method, make sure
you add an if statement that will check.

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:57.960
If we collide with another
game object whose tag is enemy.

00:04:57.961 --> 00:05:00.976
And we have a power
up, then we'll print out

00:05:00.988 --> 00:05:04.360
this log statement to
tell us that we've collided

00:05:04.361 --> 00:05:07.240
with an enemy while
our power up is activated.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:13.880
Now it's your turn.